tems • Updated Feb 7 • 5 L194: \nL195: cite71†VLA Collection 19 items • Updated Jul 17 • 4 L196: \nL197: cite72†Browse 10 collections that include this paper L198: \nL199: [Input: Ask HuggingChat about this Paper]\nL200: \nL201: System theme\nL202: \nL203: Company\nL204: \nL205: cite73†TOS cite74†Privacy cite75†About cite76†Careers†apply.workable.com L206: \nL207: Website\nL208: cite0†Models cite1†Datasets cite2†Spaces cite6†Pricing cite4†Docs --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPaper page - VCode: a Multimodal Coding Benchmark with SVG as Symbolic Visual Representation (https://huggingface.co/papers/2511.02778)\nciteturn2366view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2365view0\",\"id\":34}); Total lines: 209\nL0: [Input: Search models, datasets, users...]\nL1: \nL2:   * cite0†Models L3:   * cite1†Datasets L4:   * cite2†Spaces L5:   * cite3†Buckets new L6:   * cite4†Docs L7:   * cite5†Enterprise L8:   * cite6†Pricing L9:   *     * Website\nL10: \nL11:       * cite7†Tasks L12:       * cite8†HuggingChat L13:       * cite9†Collections L14:       * cite10†Languages L15:       * cite11†Organizations L16:     * Community\nL17:       * cite12†Blog L18:       * cite13†Posts L19:       * cite14†Daily Papers L20:       * cite15†Hardware L21:       * cite16†Learn L22:       * cite17†Discord L23:       * cite18†Forum†discuss.huggingface.co L24:       * cite19†GitHub†github.com L25:     * Solutions\nL26: \nL27:       * cite5†Team & Enterprise L28:       * cite20†Hugging Face PRO L29:       * cite21†Enterprise Support L30:       * cite22†Inference Providers L31:       * cite23†Inference Endpoints L32:       * cite3†Storage Buckets L33: \nL34:   * * * *\nL35: \nL36:   * cite24†Log In L37:   * cite25†Sign Up L38: \nL39: cite14†Papers L40: \nL41: arxiv:2511.02778\nL42: \nL43: Copy markdown\nL44: # VCode: a Multimodal Coding Benchmark with SVG as Symbolic Visual Representation\nL45: \nL46: Published on Nov 4, 2025\nL47: \nL48: · Submitted by cite26†Kevin Lin on Nov 5, 2025\nL49: \nL50: cite27†#2 Paper of the day L51: \nL52: · cite28†Jinpeng Group L53: \nL54: cite29†Upvote 104 L55: \nL56:   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   * [Button: +96]\nL57: \nL58: Authors:\nL59: \nL60: cite30†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co cite26†Kevin Qinghong Lin ,\nL61: \nL62: cite31†Image cite32†Yuhao Zheng ,\nL63: \nL64: cite33†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co cite34†Hangyu Ran ,\nL65: \nL66: cite35†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co cite36†Dantong Zhu ,\nL67: cite37†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co cite38†Dongxing Mao ,\nL68: \nL69: [Button: Linjie Li] ,\nL70: \nL71: [Button: Philip Torr] ,\nL72: \nL73: [Button: Alex Jinpeng Wang]\nL74: ## Abstract\nL75: \nL76: VCode introduces a benchmark for generating SVG code from images to preserve symbolic meaning, highlighting gaps in visual-centric coding and proposing VCoder to improve performance.\nL77: \nL78: Generated by cite39†Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct L79: Code has emerged as a precise and executable medium for reasoning and action in the agent era. Yet, progress has largely focused on language-centric tasks such as program synthesis and debugging, leaving visual-centric coding underexplored. Inspired by how humans reason over sketches, we advocate cite40†SVG code as a compact, interpretable, and executable visual representation.\nL80: We introduce cite41†VCode , a benchmark that reframes cite42†multimodal understanding as code generation: given an image, a model must produce cite40†SVG that preserves symbolic meaning for downstream reasoning. cite41†VCode covers three domains - general commonsense (MM-Vet), professional disciplines (MMMU), and visual-centric perception (CV-Bench).\nL81: To assess symbolic fidelity, we propose cite43†CodeVQA , a novel evaluation protocol in which a policy model answers questions over rendered cite40†SVG s; correct answers indicate faithful symbolic preservation. Empirically, frontier cite44†VLMs struggle to generate faithful cite40†SVG s, revealing a persistent gap between language-centric and visual-centric coding.\nL82: To close this gap, we introduce cite45†VCoder , an agentic framework that augments cite44†VLMs along two axes: (i) cite46†Thinking with Revision , which iteratively analyzes discrepancies and refines cite40†SVG code; and (ii) cite47†Acting with Visual Tools , where detectors and parsers supply structured cues such as objects, shapes, and text beyond the model's intrinsic capacity.\nL83: Across benchmarks, frontier cite44†VLMs with strong reasoning capabilities score well overall yet remain limited in cite48†professional knowledge and 3D reasoning. cite45†VCoder delivers a 12.3-point overall gain over the top-performing Claude-4-Opus. Human studies show that both humans and cite44†VLMs perform worse on rendered cite40†SVG s, their consistency reveals the promise of symbolic visual representation. The benchmark and code are available at https://github.com/CSU-JPG/cite41†VCode .\nL84: cite49†View arXiv page†arxiv.org cite50†View PDF†arxiv.org cite51†Project page†csu-jpg.github.io cite52†GitHub 136†github.com cite29†Add to collection L85: ### Community\nL86: \nL87: cite30†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co L88: \nL89: cite26†KevinQHLin L90: \nL91: Paper author Paper submitter cite53†Nov 5, 2025 L92: \nL93: TL;DR: SVG code as Symbolic Visual Representation\nL94: Project Page: cite51†https://csu-jpg.github.io/VCode/†csu-jpg.github.io L95: Github: cite52†https://github.com/CSU-JPG/VCode†github.com L96: \nL97: 🔥\nL98: \nL99: 7\nL100: \nL101: 7\nL102: \nL103: +\nL104: \nL105: Reply\nL106: \nL107: cite54†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co L108: \nL109: cite55†MRiabov L110: \nL111: cite56†Nov 13, 2025 L112: It's pretty crazy that we can generate extremely high-quality and high-resolution 3d meshes using e.g. Hunyan 3d, but struggle to generate simple representative SVGs. It shouldn't be this way, something is wrong.\nL113: Of course, Hunyan is a diffusion model, and was trained on a corpus of millions of 3d meshes, and it's evident that LLMs were not trained on SVG generation at all. But it's too big of a gap in performance.\nL114: \nL115: 🔥\nL116: \nL117: 1\nL118: \nL119: 1\nL120: \nL121: +\nL122: \nL123: Reply\nL124: \nL125: cite57†Image L126: \nL127: cite58†grantsing L128: \nL129: cite59†Nov 13, 2025 L130: \nL131: •\nL132: cite59†edited Nov 13, 2025 L133: \nL134: arXiv explained breakdown of this paper 👉 cite60†https://arxivexplained.com/papers/vcode-a-multimodal-coding-benchmark-with-svg-as-symbolic-visual-representation†arxivexplained.com L135: \nL136: [Button: See translation]\nL137: \nL138: 🔥\nL139: \nL140: 1\nL141: \nL142: 1\nL143: \nL144: +\nL145: \nL146: Reply\nL147: \nL148: deleted\nL149: \nL150: cite61†Dec 23, 2025 L151: \nL152: •\nL153: \nL154: This comment has been hidden\nL155: \nL156: [Button: Edit][Button: Preview]\nL157: \nL158: Upload images, audio, and videos by dragging in the text input, pasting, or clicking here.\nL159: \nL160: Tap or paste here to upload images\nL161: \nL162: Comment\nL163: cite62†Sign up or cite29†log in to comment\nL164: \nL165: Get this paper in your agent:\nL166: \nL167: `hf papers read 2511.02778`\nL168: \nL169: Don't have the latest CLI?\nL170: \nL171: `curl -LsSf https://hf.co/cli/install.sh | bash`\nL172: ## Models citing this paper 0\nL173: \nL174: No model linking this paper\nL175: \nL176: Cite arxiv.org/abs/2511.02778 in a model README.md to link it from this page.\nL177: \nL178: ## Datasets citing this paper 0\nL179: \nL180: No dataset linking this paper\nL181: \nL182: Cite arxiv.org/abs/2511.02778 in a dataset README.md to link it from this page.\nL183: \nL184: ### Spaces citing this paper 1\nL185: \nL186: cite63†CSU-JPG/VCode L187: ## Collections including this paper 10\nL188: \nL189: cite64†VisionLM Collection 1954 items • Updated Aug 20 • 151 L190: \nL191: cite65†AI Paper of the Day Collection A collection of papers that I think are interesting, one added each day • 641 items • Updated Jun 14 • 99 L192: \nL193: cite66†Multimodal Benchmarks Collection 254 items • Updated Feb 7 • 29 L194: \nL195: cite67†Code Collection 86 items • Updated about 11 hours ago • 7 L196: \nL197: cite68†Browse 10 collections that include this paper L198: \nL199: [Input: Ask HuggingChat about this Paper]\nL200: \nL201: System theme\nL202: \nL203: Company\nL204: cite69†TOS cite70†Privacy cite71†About cite72†Careers†apply.workable.com L205: \nL206: Website\nL207: \nL208: cite0†Models cite1†Datasets cite2†Spaces cite6†Pricing cite4†Docs --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPaper page - Shorter but not Worse: Frugal Reasoning via Easy Samples as Length Regularizers in Math RLVR (https://huggingface.co/papers/2511.01937)\nciteturn2366view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2365view0\",\"id\":63}); Total lines: 161\nL0: [Input: Search models, datasets, users...]\nL1: \nL2:   * cite0†Models L3:   * cite1†Datasets L4:   * cite2†Spaces L5:   * cite3†Buckets new L6:   * cite4†Docs L7:   * cite5†Enterprise L8:   * cite6†Pricing L9:   *     * Website\nL10: \nL11:       * cite7†Tasks L12:       * cite8†HuggingChat L13:       * cite9†Collections L14:       * cite10†Languages L15:       * cite11†Organizations L16:     * Community\nL17:       * cite12†Blog L18:       * cite13†Posts L19:       * cite14†Daily Papers L20:       * cite15†Hardware L21:       * cite16†Learn L22:       * cite17†Discord L23:       * cite18†Forum†discuss.huggingface.co L24:       * cite19†GitHub†github.com L25:     * Solutions\nL26: \nL27:       * cite5†Team & Enterprise L28:       * cite20†Hugging Face PRO L29:       * cite21†Enterprise Support L30:       * cite22†Inference Providers L31:       * cite23†Inference Endpoints L32:       * cite3†Storage Buckets L33: \nL34:   * * * *\nL35: \nL36:   * cite24†Log In L37:   * cite25†Sign Up L38: \nL39: cite14†Papers L40: \nL41: arxiv:2511.01937\nL42: \nL43: Copy markdown\nL44: # Shorter but not Worse: Frugal Reasoning via Easy Samples as Length Regularizers in Math RLVR\nL45: \nL46: Published on Nov 2, 2025\nL47: \nL48: · Submitted by cite26†Abdelaziz Bounhar on Nov 5, 2025\nL49: \nL50: · cite27†MBZUAI-IFM Paris Lab L51: \nL52: cite28†Upvote 16 L53: \nL54:   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   * [Button: +8]\nL55: \nL56: Authors:\nL57: \nL58: cite29†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co cite26†Abdelaziz Bounhar ,\nL59: \nL60: [Button: Hadi Abdine] ,\nL61: \nL62: [Button: Evan Dufraisse] ,\nL63: \nL64: cite30†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co cite31†Ahmad Chamma ,\nL65: \nL66: [Button: Amr Mohamed] ,\nL67: \nL68: [Button: Dani Bouch] ,\nL69: [Button: Michalis Vazirgiannis] ,\nL70: \nL71: cite32†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co cite33†Guokan Shang L72: ## Abstract\nL73: \nL74: Retaining and up-weighting moderately easy problems in RLVR pipelines for LLMs reduces output verbosity without explicit length penalization.\nL75: \nL76: Generated by cite34†Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct L77: cite35†Large language models (LLMs) trained for cite36†step-by-step reasoning often become excessively verbose, raising cite37†inference cost . Standard Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) pipelines filter out ``easy'' problems for training efficiency, leaving the model to train primarily on harder problems that require longer reasoning chains. This skews the cite38†output length distribution upward, resulting in a model that conflates ``cite39†thinking longer '' with ``cite40†thinking better ''.\nL78: In this work, we show that retaining and modestly up-weighting moderately easy problems acts as an implicit length regularizer. Exposing the model to solvable short-chain tasks constrains its output distribution and prevents runaway verbosity. The result is \\emph{cite41†emergent brevity for free}: the model learns to solve harder problems without inflating the output length, despite the absence of any explicit length penalization.\nL79: RLVR experiments using this approach on cite42†Qwen3-4B-Thinking-2507 (with a 16k token limit) achieve baseline cite43†pass@1 AIME25 accuracy while generating solutions that are, on average, nearly twice as short. The code is available at https://github.com/MBZUAI-Paris/Frugal-AI{GitHub}, with datasets and models on https://huggingface.co/collections/MBZUAI-Paris/k2-think-mini-68dcfa8b114686a4bd3dc2bc{Hugging Face}.\nL80: cite44†View arXiv page†arxiv.org cite45†View PDF†arxiv.org cite46†Project page†mbzuai-paris.github.io cite47†GitHub 11†github.com cite28†Add to collection L81: ### Community\nL82: \nL83: cite29†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co L84: \nL85: cite26†BounharAbdelaziz L86: \nL87: Paper author Paper submitter cite48†Nov 5, 2025 L88: \nL89: TL;DR: 🤖 Faster. Smarter. Frugal. and BETTER!\nL90: Our open-source RL-trained math model reduces verbosity by ~2× without losing accuracy (actually improving on some hard reasoning benchmarks like Omni-Hard) showing that easy problems can implicitly regularize length during RL.\nL91: \nL92: Code is publicly available on cite47†Github†github.com .\nL93: Model and Data are publicly available on cite49†Hugging Face .\nL94: \nL95: ❤️\nL96: \nL97: 1\nL98: \nL99: 1\nL100: \nL101: +\nL102: \nL103: Reply\nL104: \nL105: cite29†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co L106: \nL107: cite26†BounharAbdelaziz L108: \nL109: Paper author Paper submitter cite50†Jan 14 L110: \nL111: Update: we applied the same recipe on the 30B MoE and observed similar trends validating our approach accross model size and architectures.\nL112: \nL113: Reply\nL114: \nL115: [Button: Edit][Button: Preview]\nL116: \nL117: Upload images, audio, and videos by dragging in the text input, pasting, or clicking here.\nL118: \nL119: Tap or paste here to upload images\nL120: \nL121: Comment\nL122: cite51†Sign up or cite28†log in to comment\nL123: \nL124: Get this paper in your agent:\nL125: \nL126: `hf papers read 2511.01937`\nL127: \nL128: Don't have the latest CLI?\nL129: \nL130: `curl -LsSf https://hf.co/cli/install.sh | bash`\nL131: ## Models citing this paper 2\nL132: \nL133: cite52†MBZUAI-Paris/Frugal-Thinking-4B Text Generation • 4B • Updated Jan 12 • 2 • 7 cite53†MBZUAI-Paris/Frugal-Thinking-30B-A3B Text Generation • 31B • Updated Jan 12 • 2 L134: \nL135: ## Datasets citing this paper 1\nL136: \nL137: cite54†MBZUAI-Paris/Frugal-Thinking-RL-Data Viewer • Updated Jan 12 • 177k • 22 • 5 L138: \nL139: ### Spaces citing this paper 0\nL140: \nL141: No Space linking this paper\nL142: \nL143: Cite arxiv.org/abs/2511.01937 in a Space README.md to link it from this page.\nL144: ## Collections including this paper 2\nL145: \nL146: cite55†Frugal-Thinking Collection 13 items • Updated May 26 • 3 L147: \nL148: cite56†Frugal-AI Collection 4 items • Updated Jun 2 • 1 L149: \nL150: [Input: Ask HuggingChat about this Paper]\nL151: \nL152: System theme\nL153: \nL154: Company\nL155: \nL156: cite57†TOS cite58†Privacy cite59†About cite60†Careers†apply.workable.com L157: \nL158: Website\nL159: \nL160: cite0†Models cite1†Datasets cite2†Spaces cite6†Pricing cite4†Docs "